 {
  "key": "japan-1927:p0640_1",
  "table_no": null,
  "title": "New Investment Capacity, Estimated at ¥200 Millions",
  "en": null,
  "chapter": "Trade",
  "caption_extra": "(In million yen)",
  "parts": [
   {
    "label": null,
    "columns": [
     "Year",
     "Internal loans (million yen)",
     "Local loans (million yen)",
     "Total (million yen)",
     "Payment of stocks & net debenture increase (million yen)",
     "Additional reserves (million yen)",
     "Grand total (million yen)"
    ],
    "row_labels": [
     "1921",
     "1922",
     "1923",
     "1924",
     "1925"
    ],
    "rows": 5
   }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
   "N.B.—The “Reserves” covers joint stock and limited partnership cos. and the additions in 1924 and ’25 are presumed to be equal to that in ’23. The minor concerns of partnership organization had total paid-up capital of ¥957,000,000 at end of 1920, to advance to ¥1,355 millions three years later, an average increase of ¥130 millions. Adding this sum to the grand total mentioned in the table the investment for 1924 and ’25 comes to roughly ¥157 and 170 millions respectively. There are besides investments by individual traders and so forth. Altogether, therefore, Japan’s yearly investment may not fall below ¥200 millions, or at the rate of ¥300 per capita of annual increase of population which is about 700,000. It is interesting to compare this to the corresponding figure for England, generally put at ¥10,000 per capita."
  ]
 },
